1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
In the last video, burning methanol under a

2
00:00:02,760 --> 00:00:05,120
can of water caught only about half the

3
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:06,000
energy.

4
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:08,000
That's normal for this practical.

5
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:10,000
So how do you measure the enthalpy of

6
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:14,080
combustion of several alcohols, compare them reliably anyway,

7
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:15,920
and explain where the rest of the energy

8
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:16,560
goes?

9
00:00:17,767 --> 00:00:18,646
Here's the set-up.

10
00:00:19,127 --> 00:00:21,766
A spirit burner holds the alcohol: it's a

11
00:00:21,847 --> 00:00:23,606
small glass lamp with a wick.

12
00:00:24,167 --> 00:00:26,406
Above it, a metal can of water is

13
00:00:26,487 --> 00:00:27,926
clamped close to the flame.

14
00:00:28,487 --> 00:00:30,246
The heat has to get into the water

15
00:00:30,327 --> 00:00:32,806
through the base, and metal lets heat through

16
00:00:32,887 --> 00:00:33,926
easily.

17
00:00:34,007 --> 00:00:36,166
A draught shield blocks air currents that would

18
00:00:36,247 --> 00:00:38,566
carry heat away, and a lid cuts the

19
00:00:38,647 --> 00:00:40,566
heat lost from the water's surface.

20
00:00:41,767 --> 00:00:44,206
Measure two hundred millilitres of water into the

21
00:00:44,247 --> 00:00:46,166
can: two hundred grams.

22
00:00:46,727 --> 00:00:48,566
Weigh the burner with its cap on, and

23
00:00:48,647 --> 00:00:50,486
record the starting temperature.

24
00:00:50,807 --> 00:00:53,606
Light it, stir, and heat until the water

25
00:00:53,687 --> 00:00:55,806
is almost twenty degrees warmer.

26
00:00:56,087 --> 00:00:57,926
Cap the burner to put it out, keep

27
00:00:58,007 --> 00:01:00,486
stirring, and record the highest temperature.

28
00:01:01,047 --> 00:01:03,326
Reweigh straight away, cap on.

29
00:01:03,607 --> 00:01:04,446
Why the cap?

30
00:01:04,647 --> 00:01:07,286
Alcohol evaporates from the wick, and that lost

31
00:01:07,367 --> 00:01:09,206
mass would look like fuel burnt.

32
00:01:10,434 --> 00:01:13,473
To compare fairly, change only the alcohol.

33
00:01:13,954 --> 00:01:15,953
You measure the temperature rise and the mass

34
00:01:16,034 --> 00:01:18,033
burnt, which give the molar enthalpy.

35
00:01:18,594 --> 00:01:21,473
Keep everything else the same: the water's mass,

36
00:01:21,514 --> 00:01:24,033
the can, the flame's distance, the shield and

37
00:01:24,114 --> 00:01:26,593
lid, and the twenty degree rise.

38
00:01:26,914 --> 00:01:27,953
Why the same rise?

39
00:01:28,514 --> 00:01:31,953
Hotter water loses heat faster, so equal rises

40
00:01:32,034 --> 00:01:34,113
keep the heat lost from the water similar.

41
00:01:35,334 --> 00:01:37,253
Here's the first methanol trial.

42
00:01:37,494 --> 00:01:40,613
The water rose twenty point zero degrees.

43
00:01:40,814 --> 00:01:43,653
q is m c delta T, with m

44
00:01:43,734 --> 00:01:45,973
the mass of the water: sixteen point seven

45
00:01:46,134 --> 00:01:47,093
two kilojoules.

46
00:01:47,574 --> 00:01:50,373
The burner lost one point four zero grams,

47
00:01:50,454 --> 00:01:52,933
which is zero point zero four three seven

48
00:01:53,094 --> 00:01:54,133
moles of methanol.

49
00:01:54,534 --> 00:01:57,733
So delta H is minus q over n:

50
00:01:57,814 --> 00:02:00,293
minus three hundred and eighty three kilojoules per

51
00:02:00,374 --> 00:02:00,773
mole.

52
00:02:01,174 --> 00:02:03,893
Same steps as neutralisation, but n is now

53
00:02:03,934 --> 00:02:05,493
the moles of fuel burnt.

54
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:09,180
Do three trials for each alcohol and take

55
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:09,820
the mean.

56
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:12,620
Methanol, minus three eighty three.

57
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:15,980
Ethanol, about minus six ninety.

58
00:02:16,060 --> 00:02:19,580
Propan-one-ol, about minus nine eighty five.

59
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:22,940
Butan-one-ol, about minus twelve hundred.

60
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:25,100
The values get more negative as the chain

61
00:02:25,180 --> 00:02:27,660
gets longer, just as the data values do.

62
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:30,540
But every one is only about half its

63
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:31,500
data value.

64
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:33,740
Are they reliable?

65
00:02:34,220 --> 00:02:37,020
Each alcohol's three trials agreed within about two

66
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:38,940
percent, so yes.

67
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:42,140
And here's what makes the comparison trustworthy: the

68
00:02:42,260 --> 00:02:45,020
gap between neighbouring alcohols, two to three hundred

69
00:02:45,180 --> 00:02:47,860
kilojoules per mole, is far bigger than the

70
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:50,540
spread within any alcohol's trials.

71
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:52,860
So the order isn't a fluke, even though

72
00:02:52,940 --> 00:02:54,220
every value is low.

73
00:02:55,434 --> 00:02:56,953
But they aren't accurate.

74
00:02:57,434 --> 00:02:59,753
Follow each error through the chain, as in

75
00:02:59,834 --> 00:03:01,353
the neutralisation practical.

76
00:03:01,834 --> 00:03:04,313
Heat warms the can, the clamp and the

77
00:03:04,474 --> 00:03:06,953
air instead of the water, so q is

78
00:03:07,034 --> 00:03:07,753
too small.

79
00:03:08,394 --> 00:03:11,833
A sooty flame means incomplete combustion, so less

80
00:03:11,914 --> 00:03:14,313
energy is released, and soot on the can

81
00:03:14,474 --> 00:03:15,833
slows heat getting in.

82
00:03:16,554 --> 00:03:19,673
Alcohol that evaporates without burning makes n too

83
00:03:19,754 --> 00:03:20,153
big.

84
00:03:20,714 --> 00:03:23,273
All three make delta H less negative.

85
00:03:23,914 --> 00:03:27,033
Not every error does: recording more water than

86
00:03:27,114 --> 00:03:29,993
the can holds makes delta H too negative.

87
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:34,320
In the neutralisation practical, you extended the cooling

88
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:36,480
line back to recover lost heat.

89
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:37,520
Why not here?

90
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:40,160
That reaction was almost instant.

91
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:43,360
Here the flame heats for several minutes, losing

92
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:44,640
heat the whole time.

93
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,960
Extending the line back to when the flame

94
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:49,760
went out corrects only the last little bit.

95
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:52,040
So the real fix is to stop the

96
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:54,080
heat escaping in the first place.

97
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:57,540
So match each fix to its cause.

98
00:03:58,020 --> 00:04:00,660
Air currents carry heat away, so use a

99
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:02,380
draught shield and a lid.

100
00:04:02,660 --> 00:04:05,300
Heat spreads into the air around the flame,

101
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:07,540
so keep the flame close to the can.

102
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:11,020
Soot means too little oxygen, so trim the

103
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:13,500
wick for a smaller flame, which usually burns

104
00:04:13,540 --> 00:04:15,780
cleaner, and wipe the can's base.

105
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:19,380
Evaporation inflates the mass, so keep the cap

106
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:19,860
on.

107
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:22,820
Repeats can't fix any of these: they make

108
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:25,140
results reliable, not accurate.

109
00:04:26,367 --> 00:04:29,326
Is it valid, meaning, does it measure what

110
00:04:29,407 --> 00:04:30,206
you intended?

111
00:04:30,767 --> 00:04:34,126
For ranking the alcohols, mostly yes: only the

112
00:04:34,287 --> 00:04:36,846
alcohol changes, so the losses are similar.

113
00:04:37,407 --> 00:04:39,806
The percentages drift down a little, but that

114
00:04:39,887 --> 00:04:42,606
drift is smaller than the gaps between alcohols,

115
00:04:42,727 --> 00:04:43,966
so the order holds.

116
00:04:44,527 --> 00:04:46,846
As a measure of each enthalpy, it's less

117
00:04:46,927 --> 00:04:49,246
valid, because it captures only the heat that

118
00:04:49,327 --> 00:04:50,366
reaches the water.

119
00:04:51,567 --> 00:04:52,886
Now the risks.

120
00:04:53,167 --> 00:04:56,366
All four alcohols are flammable, and three are

121
00:04:56,447 --> 00:04:57,886
highly flammable.

122
00:04:57,967 --> 00:05:00,206
So never refill a burner near a flame

123
00:05:00,327 --> 00:05:02,446
or while it's warm, and cap it to

124
00:05:02,487 --> 00:05:03,326
put it out.

125
00:05:03,647 --> 00:05:07,486
Methanol is also toxic: swallowed, breathed in or

126
00:05:07,567 --> 00:05:10,606
through the skin, it can damage organs, including

127
00:05:10,687 --> 00:05:11,646
eyesight.

128
00:05:11,767 --> 00:05:15,886
Propan-one-ol and butan-one-ol can seriously damage eyes, so

129
00:05:15,967 --> 00:05:17,726
wear safety glasses.

130
00:05:17,807 --> 00:05:20,766
Leftover alcohol goes in the labelled organic waste

131
00:05:20,847 --> 00:05:22,606
container, not the sink.

132
00:05:23,834 --> 00:05:25,433
Two phrases to keep.

133
00:05:25,754 --> 00:05:29,753
Water in q, fuel in n: the water's

134
00:05:29,914 --> 00:05:32,393
mass goes into q, and the fuel's mass

135
00:05:32,474 --> 00:05:34,073
lost gives n.

136
00:05:34,394 --> 00:05:35,433
And for accuracy:

137
00:05:35,994 --> 00:05:38,793
shield, lid, close, cap.

138
00:05:39,274 --> 00:05:41,673
Each one fixes a named cause.

139
00:05:42,900 --> 00:05:43,460
Your turn.

140
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:46,900
An ethanol spirit burner heats two hundred grams

141
00:05:46,980 --> 00:05:47,820
of water.

142
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:50,900
The temperatures, the burner's masses and the molar

143
00:05:51,060 --> 00:05:52,180
mass are on screen.

144
00:05:52,820 --> 00:05:56,140
Calculate the molar enthalpy of combustion of ethanol.

145
00:05:56,420 --> 00:05:57,700
Pause here to answer.

146
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:11,220
Delta T is eighteen point six degrees.

147
00:06:11,540 --> 00:06:14,820
q uses the water: about fifteen thousand five

148
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:16,460
hundred and fifty joules.

149
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:19,620
The burner lost one point zero six grams,

150
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:22,420
so n is zero point zero two three

151
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:23,700
zero moles.

152
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:26,340
Delta H is minus six hundred and seventy

153
00:06:26,420 --> 00:06:28,180
six kilojoules per mole.

154
00:06:28,420 --> 00:06:30,500
Putting the fuel's mass into q is the

155
00:06:30,580 --> 00:06:33,460
classic slip: it gives about minus three point

156
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:34,220
six.

157
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:36,820
Water in q, fuel in n.

158
00:06:38,034 --> 00:06:38,673
Last one.

159
00:06:39,234 --> 00:06:42,513
A student burns methanol and butan-one-ol under an

160
00:06:42,594 --> 00:06:46,033
uncovered copper can, with no draught shield.

161
00:06:46,074 --> 00:06:49,313
Her results, on screen, are far less negative

162
00:06:49,354 --> 00:06:50,993
than the data values.

163
00:06:51,114 --> 00:06:53,953
Explain why, and suggest one improvement that would

164
00:06:54,194 --> 00:06:55,633
reduce the difference.

165
00:06:55,794 --> 00:06:56,913
Pause here to answer.

166
00:07:07,334 --> 00:07:10,213
With no shield and no lid, heat escapes

167
00:07:10,254 --> 00:07:12,133
to the air and the can instead of

168
00:07:12,214 --> 00:07:12,773
the water.

169
00:07:13,254 --> 00:07:15,653
So the measured q is too small, and

170
00:07:15,734 --> 00:07:17,973
delta H comes out less negative.

171
00:07:18,534 --> 00:07:20,533
Improve it with a draught shield and a

172
00:07:20,654 --> 00:07:22,613
lid, so more of the heat stays with

173
00:07:22,654 --> 00:07:23,173
the water.

174
00:07:23,814 --> 00:07:27,013
Repeating it more times isn't the fix: repeats

175
00:07:27,094 --> 00:07:30,613
improve reliability, but heat escapes in every run.

176
00:07:31,834 --> 00:07:35,273
So: change only the alcohol, put the water's

177
00:07:35,354 --> 00:07:37,993
mass in q and the fuel's in n,

178
00:07:38,194 --> 00:07:40,553
trace each error to q or n, and

179
00:07:40,634 --> 00:07:42,393
stop heat loss at its source.

